WEBVTT

NOTE Ruwaidix source-grounded narration; original owner archive and labeled explanations.

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:07.021
In 2016, we met around an organic chemistry bench. Myrrh, nutmeg, extraction questions,

00:00:07.021 --> 00:00:13.521
and the suspicion that a traditional medicinal claim needed more than an interesting smell.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:21.916
It was easy to obtain an extract and much harder to say what it

00:00:21.916 --> 00:00:27.313
contained. Identifying a constituent would still be different from proving a useful biological effect.

00:00:29.978 --> 00:00:36.089
The group that became Chem Squad included seven chemistry graduates. There was no grand

00:00:36.089 --> 00:00:42.200
institution behind us. We kept bringing papers, arguments, and new questions back to the conversation.

00:00:45.103 --> 00:00:51.034
Around that period, our student chapter of the American Chemical Society at Qatar University

00:00:51.034 --> 00:00:56.964
was creating a different kind of laboratory: one open to more students and the public.

00:00:59.812 --> 00:01:06.765
We organized demonstrations and scientific discussions. Through chapter activities, twenty-nine students had opportunities

00:01:06.765 --> 00:01:13.719
to train on instruments. The work was collective; the photographs deserve collective credit.

00:01:16.853 --> 00:01:22.789
Chemistry festivals taught me another form of precision: speaking clearly when the audience has

00:01:22.789 --> 00:01:28.725
every reason to question you, and resisting the urge to make a demonstration seem conclusive.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:37.477
As years passed, we moved into different specialties. Some conversations continued through weekly meetings,

00:01:37.477 --> 00:01:43.433
and some through disagreements sent across a phone long after a class had ended.

00:01:46.228 --> 00:01:51.994
There is no final group photograph that explains all of it. What the archive

00:01:51.994 --> 00:01:57.759
shows is simpler: people kept making time for scientific curiosity, even when nobody assigned it.

